        },
        // The match start's capture read in RedactionCandidate::edit() is
        // the one integer the increment can rewrite without any
        // observable difference: the candidate grammar anchors the whole
        // match at the key's first byte, so $match[0][1] (the match start)
        // and $match[1][1] (the key start) carry the same value for every
        // possible match, and the edit's start answer cannot change
        // (verified by running the full suite under the manually applied
        // mutation -- the complete suite passes). The decrement direction
        // stays under the mutator: it reads the undefined capture -1, the
        // engine answers an undefined-dimension error, and tests kill it.
        // The ignore is scoped to that single source line.
        //
        // The two literal `2`s in CsrfCookieValue::from()'s pair
        // split are the next integers the increment can rewrite without
        // any observable difference: raising explode's limit to 3 only
        // matters for a value carrying a second `=`, and the token
        // grammar (base64url body, hex signature) admits no `=` at all,
        // so every such value is invalid under both spellings and is
        // refused identically; raising array_pad's size to 3 only appends
        // an element the destructuring never reads, for every pair shape.
        // The decrement direction stays under the mutator on both
        // literals: a limit of 1 reads the whole pair as the key, so the
        // cookie answers absent and the issuance test kills it, and a
        // pad size of 1 truncates the value arm, so the destructuring
        // warns and the suite fails on warnings. The ignore is scoped to
        // that single source line.
        // The literal `2` in CsrfTokenManager::isValid()'s array_pad is
        // the next integer the increment can rewrite without any
        // observable difference: raising the pad size to 3 only appends
        // an arm the destructuring never reads, for every parts shape
        // (explode with a limit answers one or two arms, and padding a
        // two-arm list to three adds the unread third). The decrement
        // direction stays under the mutator: a pad size of 1 truncates
        // the signature arm, the empty-string padding stops arriving,
        // and the shape-gate test on the separator-less token kills it.
        // The explode limit two lines up stays under the mutator too:
        // raising that limit to 3 splits the signature at the second
        // dot, the trailing-suffix test kills it. This ignore is scoped
        // to that single source line.
        //
        // The clamp floor's literal `0` in ResponseTime::fromElapsedNs()
        // is the next integer the increment can rewrite without any
        // observable difference: raising the floor from 0 to 1 changes
        // the clamped span by at most one nanosecond, and the header's
        // two-decimal render resolves five thousand nanoseconds
        // (0.005ms) -- a zero span and a one-nanosecond span both
        // answer `0.00ms`, a negative delta clamps to those same two
        // values under either floor, and every span above one
        // nanosecond is untouched by the floor (verified by running
        // the full suite under the manually applied mutation -- the
        // complete suite passes). The decrement direction stays under
        // the mutator: a floor of -1 clamps a negative delta to -1ns,
        // sprintf keeps the sign through the zero, and the header
        // answers `-0.00ms`, which the broken-clock test kills. The
        // clamp call's removal stays under the FunctionCallRemoval
        // mutator on the same line: the negative delta renders as
        // `-0.25ms`, and the same test kills it. The division's own
        // literal lives on the constant's declaration line, not this
        // one, so the exact-span matrix keeps it under this mutator.
        // This ignore is scoped to that single source line.
        //
        // The default retry policy's delay cap in
        // BatchSpanProcessor::__construct() is the next integer the
        // increment can rewrite without any observable difference,
        // and the reachability is the whole reason: the shipped
        // default policy is the zero-retry posture, so shouldRetry()
        // answers false for every index a failed attempt can carry,
        // the drain breaks before delayMs() is ever consulted, and no
        // default-constructed processor can read the ceiling at all
        // -- the composition seam wires the deployment's own policy
        // explicitly, and the suite's retry tests construct theirs
        // (verified by running the full suite under the manually
        // applied mutation -- the complete suite passes). The
        // neighbouring queue and batch bounds stay under the mutator:
        // the default-bounds test kills those. This ignore is scoped
        // to that single source line.
        "IncrementInteger": {
            "ignoreSourceCodeByRegex": [
                // The Accept-negotiation sentinel and the range-specificity
                // weights are value-equivalent under the integer mutators:
                // the quality grammar admits only non-negative values, so
                // max(0.0, q) answers q for every reachable quality and the
                // -0/-2 sentinels keep the reset and tie arms identical; the
                // surviving specificity literals either preserve the
                // concrete > subtype-wildcard > wildcard order without
                // creating a tie (the +1/+2 rewrites) or land the wildcard
                // on the sentinel itself where the tie arm's max(0.0, q)
                // answers the reset's q (the -1 rewrite) -- and the
                // equal-specificity ties that WOULD differ are killed by
                // the negotiation battery's application/*;q=0 series. The
                // produce split's explode limit only reshuffles bytes the
                // destructure never reads: the produces grammar admits
                // exactly one slash, so limits of two and three split the
                // same valid spellings into the same [type, subtype] pair.
                ".*\\$bestSpecificity = -1;.*",
                ".*\\? 0 : 1.*\\? 0 : 1.*",
                ".*explode\\('/', \\$produce, 2\\).*",
                "\\$matchStart = \\$match\\[0\\]\\[1\\];",
                "\\[\\$body, \\$signature\\] = array_pad\\(\\$parts, 2, ''\\);",
                "\\[\\$key, \\$value\\] = array_pad\\(explode\\('=', mb_trim\\(\\$pair\\), 2\\), 2, ''\\);",
                // The cookie bag fold's pair split now spells its two
                // literals on two lines so each carries its own
                // verdict: the array_pad size is the value-equivalent
                // one (padding a two-arm list to three appends an arm
                // the destructuring never reads, for every pair shape
                // the explode above can hand it), while the explode
                // limit on its own line stays UNDER the mutator -- the
                // bag's values can legally carry '=' (pinned by the
                // value-with-equals test), so raising that limit to
                // three would truncate "token=b=c" at the second '='
                // and change the bag, and the kill is owned. The
                // decrement directions stay under the mutator on both
                // lines for the same reasons the CSRF entry spells.
                // This ignore is scoped to that single source line.
                "\\[\\$name, \\$value\\] = array_pad\\(\\$parts, 2, ''\\);",
                // The concurrency flag's comment-split explode limit: the
                // read takes element [0], which is the substring before the
                // first '#' for every limit of two or more -- the same
                // equivalence the produce split above documents.
                ".*explode\\('#', \\$spelling, 2\\).*",
                "\\$milliseconds = max\\(0, \\$elapsedNs\\) / self::NANOSECONDS_PER_MILLISECOND;",
                "private readonly RetryBackoffPolicy \\$retryBackoffPolicy = new RetryBackoffPolicy\\(0, 0, 0\\),",
                "RetryBackoffPolicy \\$retryBackoffPolicy = new RetryBackoffPolicy\\(0, 0, 0\\),",
                // AcceptHeaderParser::mediaRange()'s explode limit is the
                // next integer the increment can rewrite without any
                // observable difference: the read takes element [0], and
                // for every limit of two or more the first element is the
                // substring before the first separator, so raising the
                // limit to three only reshuffles bytes the read never
                // touches (verified by running the parser battery under
                // the manually applied mutation -- the complete battery
                // passes). The ignore is scoped to that single source
                // line.
                ".*explode\\(';', \\$part, 2\\)\\[0\\].*",
                // ReplayBodyCapture::capture()'s media-type split limit is
                // the next integer the increment can rewrite without any
                // observable difference, by the same construction the
                // AcceptHeaderParser::mediaRange() entry above argues: the
                // read takes element [0], and for every limit of two or more
                // the first element is the substring before the first
                // semicolon, so raising the limit only reshuffles bytes the
                // read never touches (verified by running the full suite
                // under the manually applied mutation -- the complete suite
                // passes, the parameterised header fixture included). The
                // decrement direction stays under the mutator: a limit of 1
                // returns the whole parameterised line as the media type,
                // the gate misses it, and the parameterised header test
                // kills it. This ignore is scoped to that single source
                // line.
                ".*explode\\(';', \\$contentType, 2\\)\\[0\\].*"
            ]
        },
        // The scan resume's capture read in RedactedMessage::of() is the
        // one integer the decrement can rewrite without any observable
        // difference: the separator region between the key and the value
        // can host no candidate start (its bytes are whitespace and one
        // [=:], never a key byte), so resuming at the quote capture's
        // offset -- the value region's first byte -- and resuming at the
        // separator capture's offset select the identical next candidate
        // for every possible message (verified by running the full suite
        // under the manually applied mutation -- the complete suite
        // passes). The increment direction stays under the mutator: it
        // reads the undefined capture 4, the engine answers an
        // undefined-dimension error, and tests kill it. The ignore is
        // scoped to that single source line.
        "DecrementInteger": {
            // The replay window's default literal: the knob's only
            // observable face is the TTL the round-up hands the store, and
            // the round-up of 299 999 ms lands on the same 300 seconds the
            // default itself rounds to (intdiv(300 998, 1000) = 300), so
            // the decremented default is value-equivalent through every
            // verdict the protector can answer. The incremented default
            // stays under the mutator: its TTL lands on 301, and the
            // default-ctor TTL test kills it. Scoped to that single
            // method.
            "ignore": [
                "Zef\\Framework\\Http\\ApcuReplayProtector::__construct"
            ],
            "ignoreSourceCodeByRegex": [
                // The Accept-negotiation sentinel and the range-specificity
                // weights carry the IncrementInteger entry's construction
                // verbatim (non-negative qualities make the sentinel and
                // tie arms identical; the surviving literal rewrites
                // preserve the specificity order without creating a tie,
                // and the equal-specificity ties that would differ are
                // killed by the negotiation battery).
                ".*\\$bestSpecificity = -1;.*",
                ".*\\? 0 : 1.*\\? 0 : 1.*",
                "\\$offset = \\$match\\[3\\]\\[1\\];",
                // ZefException::defaultErrorCode()'s short-name slice start
                // is the next integer the decrement can rewrite without any
                // observable difference: the -1 window only re-includes the
                // namespace separator byte, and the derivation's own wash
                // replaces every non-[A-Z0-9_] byte with the underscore and
                // its two-sided trim then erases that leading underscore,
                // so the derived code is byte-identical for every class
                // name (verified by running the exception battery under the
                // manually applied mutation, with the multibyte-namespace
                // and overlong digit-name fixtures in play -- the complete
                // battery passes). The neighbouring arithmetic stays under
                // the mutator: the byte-scale separator read and the byte
                // slice are killed by the same fixtures, and the truncation
                // bound by the 64-character one. The ignore is scoped to
                // that single source line.
                "\\$shortName = \\$separator === false \\? \\$class : mb_substr\\(\\$class, \\$separator \\+ 1\\);",
                // The browser factory's flat query-count fallback is the
                // one integer the decrement can rewrite without any
                // observable difference: every consumer of the count
                // clamps it at zero before rendering -- the HTML page's
                // own profiler row reads max(0, $queryCount) and the
                // debug toolbar's markup the same -- so a fallback of
                // minus one renders as the identical "DB queries: 0"
                // (verified by running the factory battery under the
                // manually applied mutation -- the complete battery
                // passes). The neighbouring fallbacks stay under the
                // mutator: the duration's one-zero rewrite and the
                // exception count's decrement are both visible through
                // the rendered page and the tests kill them. The ignore
                // is scoped to that single source line.
                "\\$errorRequestContext instanceof ErrorRequestContext \\? \\$errorRequestContext->queryCount\\(\\) : 0,",
                // The toolbar's flat count fallbacks are the two integers
                // the decrement can rewrite without any observable
                // difference: the markup the injector renders reads both
                // counts through their own zero clamps -- the queries row
                // answers max(0, $queries) and the exceptions row
                // max(0, $exceptions) -- so fallbacks of minus one render
                // as the identical "DB queries: 0" and "Exceptions: 0"
                // (verified by running the toolbar battery under the
                // manually applied mutations -- the complete battery
                // passes, the no-context page still shows both zeroes).
                // The increment direction stays under the mutator: it
                // lifts each fallback to one and the battery kills it,
                // and the factory's own query-count fallback carries its
                // own ignore one entry above. The ignore is scoped to
                // these two source lines.
                "\\$context instanceof ErrorRequestContext \\? \\$context->(queryCount|exceptionCount)\\(\\) : 0,",
                // The opened-file size clamp's lower bound is the one
                // integer the decrement can rewrite without any
                // observable difference: the guard two statements
                // earlier has already proven the stream is the real
                // file at the path (same device, same inode, no link),
                // and the engine's stat contract answers a regular
                // file's size as a non-negative count on every
                // supported platform -- so max(0, size) and
                // max(-1, size) agree on every reachable input
                // (verified by running the security battery under the
                // manually applied mutation -- the complete battery
                // passes, the empty-file rollback offset still reads
                // zero). The increment direction stays under the
                // mutator: max(1, size) lifts an empty file's rollback
                // offset to one and the battery kills it. The ignore
                // is scoped to that single source line.
                "return max\\(0, \\$opened\\['size'\\]\\);"
            ]
        },
        // The settled memo's early return in
        // DefinitionCycles::visit() (moved out of ServiceDefinitionGraph
        // when the graph became the three-verdict orchestrator) is the one
        // return whose removal
        // is value-equivalent: the walk is a pure function of the edge
        // map, so re-visiting a settled node recomputes the identical
        // acyclic verdict -- the memo is a performance guard, never a
        // correctness branch, and the cycle detector's chain test is
        // independent of it (verified by running the full suite under the
        // manually applied mutation -- the complete suite passes, cycles
        // still rejected, shared dependencies still accepted). The throw
        // and the recursion in the same method stay under their mutators:
        // tests kill those. This ignore is scoped to that single method.
        //
        // The valid-encoding early return in
        // TelemetrySanitizer::scrubbed() is the same class of guard:
        // mb_scrub() over a string that already carries valid UTF-8
        // answers the identical bytes, so the fallthrough recomputes
        // the same value the early return carried (verified by running
        // the full suite under the manually applied mutation -- the
        // complete suite passes, the invalid-byte scrub still lands).
        // The early return is a performance guard on the hot clamp
        // path, never a correctness branch. This ignore is scoped to
        // that single method.
        // The divergence guard's early return rides the same NaN-false
        // property as the LogicalOr zone above: removing the return
        // falls a NaN series through to the abs() comparison, which
        // answers false on the NaN operand -- the identical answer the
        // guard spells. This ignore is scoped to that single method.
        //
        // The memo-hit fast path in ServiceResolutions::resolve() is the
        // fourth zone, and the one this entry grew for: its fall-through
        // lands on the singleton lane's own answer-or-build seam, and
        // SingletonMemo::answer() re-checks the memo before it ever
        // consults the build closure, so removing the return answers the
        // identical memoised instance through the longer road -- the two
        // spellings differ only in the fast path's skipped closure
        // allocation and lifetime read, which is exactly why the line
        // exists (the measured hot ride: ScopeResolutionBench answers
        // ~0.52us with the fast path against ~0.74us without it).
        // Verified by running the full suite under the manually applied
        // mutation (the complete suite passes) and by construction: the
        // only state that could tell the two apart -- a memoised id with
        // no registration behind it -- is unreachable through the public
        // surface (set() forgets the memo on every re-registration, and
        // the memo only fills for registered ids). Scoped by source
        // regex to that single line; the neighbouring `return match`
        // under the same mutator stays live and killed (its removal
        // answers null, and the identity-through-a-scope test kills
        // it). NB: this entry is the file's ONLY ReturnRemoval key --
        // a duplicate key silently disarms every ignore listed here
        // (the gate-round on PR #396 learned this the hard way).
        // The injector's early null return is unreachable-in-effect
        // behind the try's own Throwable net: removing it hands the null
        // spliced document to Stream::fromString(), whose string
        // parameter refuses the null with a TypeError under strict types,
        // and the catch that exists for exactly those refusals answers
        // the same null the guard answered (verified by running the
        // toolbar batteries under the manually applied mutation -- the
        // complete batteries pass, the unclosed-document passthrough
        // included). The guard itself stays: the analysers read the
        // narrowed string the try's build needs. The method scope is the
        // engine's narrowest for this mutator; the try's own return
        // stays under the mutator and its removal is killed by every
        // injection test, though the engine no longer asks it here.
        "ReturnRemoval": {
            "ignore": [
                "Zef\\Framework\\Container\\DefinitionCycles::visit",
                "Zef\\Framework\\Middleware\\DebugToolbarInjector::updatedBody",
                "Zef\\Framework\\Telemetry\\TelemetrySanitizer::scrubbed",
                "Zef\\Framework\\Telemetry\\PrometheusNumbers::diverged",
                // The router lane's early answers are owned by the
                // next guard or the same computation: RadixTree::match()'s
                // walk-empty and host-empty returns flow to the identical
                // not-found verdict (host selection of an empty candidate
                // list answers the empty agnostic bucket, and the version
                // guard behind it answers empty the same way), the version
                // selection's null-requestVersion return flows to a final
                // filter that answers [] over the all-versioned candidates
                // anyway, LastModifiedMatch::satisfied()'s absent-header
                // false flows into a strtotime('') that answers false on
                // the same arm, and the pattern bucket's reset return
                // falls into an equality arm that appends the winner a
                // second time -- unobservable behind every consumer's
                // first-match-wins walk and de-duplicated method union.
                "Zef\\Framework\\Config\\Routing\\RadixTree::match",
                "Zef\\Framework\\Config\\Routing\\RouteVersionSelection::select",
                "Zef\\Framework\\Config\\Routing\\RouteHostPatternBucket::offer",
                "Zef\\Framework\\Middleware\\LastModifiedMatch::satisfied",
                // The proxy matcher's exact-string arm is subsumed by its
                // own packed-form arm: matches() validates the address
                // under judgement before the walk ever consults an
                // entry, so a string-equal entry always packs to the
                // identical non-false binary the second arm compares,
                // and removing the fast path's return true answers true
                // through the packed equality one line later (verified
                // by construction: entry === ip implies
                // packed(entry) === packed(ip), and a validated ip
                // never packs false). The packed arm's own return and
                // the CIDR delegation stay under the mutator: their
                // removals are killed by the mapped-spelling and
                // CIDR batteries. Scoped to that single method.
                "Zef\\Framework\\Http\\TrustedProxyMatcher::entryMatches"
            ],
            "ignoreSourceCodeByRegex": [
                "return \\$this->singletonMemo->instance\\(\\$id\\);"
            ]
        },
        // The metrics recorder's null-safe increment is unreachable-in-
        // effect behind the recorder's own Throwable net: removing the
        // null-safe operator hands the null meter a method call that
        // throws a TypeError, and the catch whose whole contract is
        // "metrics are best-effort and must never replace the original
        // failure" answers the same silent no-op the operator answered
        // (verified by running the mapping battery under the manually
        // applied mutation -- the complete battery passes, the
        // metrics-disabled posture included). This ignore is scoped to
        // that single method.
        "NullSafeMethodCall": {
            "ignore": [
                "Zef\\Framework\\Middleware\\ErrorFaultMetricsRecorder::record"
            ]
        },
        // The two problem-document encoders carry the substitution flag
        // no input can ever exercise: every string either writer's map
        // carries is valid UTF-8 by upstream construction -- the fault
        // record's message and path leave their clamps through mb_substr,
        // which replaces invalid bytes with the ASCII question mark before
        // the record is built, its class name, code, method and identifiers
        // pass their own ASCII-or-UTF-8 grammars, and the problem
        // document's detail travels the same mb_substr clamp while its
        // type, title, code, trace id and details answer fixed literals
        // or grammar-validated spellings -- so the substitution arm the
        // bitwise rewrite would drop never fires, and the throwing flag's
        // removal changes nothing an encoder that cannot fail could
        // observe (verified by running the mapping battery under the
        // manually applied mutations -- the complete battery passes,
        // the invalid-UTF-8 fixtures included: the broken bytes surface
        // as question marks upstream of both writers). These ignores are
        // scoped to those two methods.
        //
        // The durable books' and journal's own records join the same
        // class: the attribution record, the quarantine record and the
        // drain trace are integers and grammar-bound ASCII by
        // construction (the hex entry identity, the letter-first
        // listener identity, and the five integer counts), so
        // JSON_PRESERVE_ZERO_FRACTION has no float to preserve and
        // JSON_THROW_ON_ERROR has no failure to throw -- the bitwise
        // rewrite answers byte-identical records for every value the
        // vocabularies can ever hand the encoders. These ignores are
        // scoped to those three methods.
        "BitwiseOr": {
            "ignore": [
                "Zef\\Framework\\Http\\ErrorFaultLogWriter::write",
                "Zef\\Framework\\Http\\ProblemDetailsJsonResponseFactory::create",
                "Zef\\Framework\\Adapters\\Event\\DeliveryAttributionEncoder::encode",
                "Zef\\Framework\\Adapters\\Event\\DeliveryQuarantineLane::quarantineListener",
                "Zef\\Framework\\Event\\DrainTraceEncoder::encode"
            ]
        },
        // The correlation pair's tolerant extraction is a chain of
        // small refusals whose every remaining return is load-bearing:
        // the line's oversize refusal is killed by the whitespace-
        // padding test (a padded line that trims to the exact width
        // would otherwise ship), the width selection carries the
        // value the grammar reads (its removal answers the implicit
        // null the happy-path test refuses), and the companion's own
        // bound is the constructor's to enforce -- the fail-fast read
        // the reference carried ahead of it was proven
        // unreachable-in-effect behind that identical bound (verified
        // by running the full suite under the manually applied
        // mutation -- the complete suite passes, the at-the-bound
        // companions and the malformed parents still refused), so
        // the port drops it rather than ignoring its removal.
        "PregMatchRemoveCaret": {
            "ignore": [
                "Zef\\Framework\\Telemetry\\CorrelationInboundPair::identityFromLine"
            ]
        },
        // The width selection ahead of the grammar makes the anchors'
        // removal value-equivalent from the other side: a match that
        // starts past the head or ends before the tail needs a line
        // longer than the fifty-five characters the width selection
        // already demanded, so the unanchored match cannot select
        // anything the anchored one refused (verified by running the
        // full suite under the manually applied mutations -- the
        // complete suite passes). The trace-context pair's own anchors
        // stay under the mutator: it has no width guard, and the
        // leading-artefact and over-long line tests kill both there.
        // This ignore is scoped to that single method.
        "PregMatchRemoveDollar": {
            "ignore": [
                "Zef\\Framework\\Telemetry\\CorrelationInboundPair::identityFromLine"
            ]
        },
        // The identity's hard wire-size ceiling is the one bound the
        // GreaterThan mutator can narrow without any observable
        // difference, because the ceiling is unreachable by
        // construction: the tracestate's own 512-byte bound caps the
        // propagation pair at 568 bytes, and no identity the
        // constructor accepts can ever read 8192 at the ceiling's line
        // -- the bound is the reference's own defense-in-depth against
        // a future companion bound rising past it, and the mutator's
        // narrowing moves a ceiling nothing can reach (verified by
        // running the full suite under the manually applied mutation
        // -- the complete suite passes). The tracestate bound's own
        // GreaterThan mutant in validateTraceState() stays under the
        // mutator: the at-the-ceiling companion test kills it. This
        // ignore is scoped to the constructor alone.
        // The infinity's sign judgement in
        // PrometheusNumbers::rendered() is the next GreaterThan zone:
        // the branch only runs on values is_finite() already refused,
        // and zero is finite -- so the non-finite domain the judgement
        // reads excludes zero by construction, and > and >= agree on
        // every value that can ever reach the line (+Inf and -Inf are
        // sign-stable under both comparisons). This ignore is scoped
        // to that single method.
        "GreaterThan": {
            "ignore": [
                "Zef\\Framework\\Telemetry\\CorrelationIdentity::__construct",
                "Zef\\Framework\\Telemetry\\PrometheusNumbers::rendered"
            ],
            // The retry policy's final headroom check in
            // readyAtForNextAttempt() is value-equivalent under the
            // GreaterThan boundary: when the delay lands exactly on the
            // headroom, the uncapped spelling answers now + headroom,
            // and headroom is PHP_INT_MAX - now BY CONSTRUCTION, so the
            // addition answers PHP_INT_MAX -- the identical value the
            // capped spelling returns. No test can distinguish the two
            // spellings on any input the method accepts (now >= 0 keeps
            // the headroom arithmetic inside the integer range; verified
            // by running the family suite under the manually applied
            // mutation -- the complete suite passes, the halving-boundary
            // test included). The LOOP guard's own GreaterThan mutants
            // stay under the mutator: the overflow-window and
            // exact-halving boundary tests kill them. Scoped by regex to
            // the final check's line alone so the loop guard keeps its
            // boundary protection.
            "ignoreSourceCodeByRegex": [
                ".*if \\(\\$delay > \\$headroom\\) \\{.*"
            ]
        },
        // The system clock's int cast in
        // HealthScrapeClock::__construct() is value-equivalent by the
        // platform: hrtime(true) answers int on every 64-bit build
        // (the only builds the framework declares), so the cast is
        // the analyser's narrowing of an int|float stub, never a
        // runtime coercion (verified by running the family suite
        // under the manually applied mutation -- the complete suite
        // passes). ErrorRequestContext::__construct()'s start stamp
        // carries the same platform fact for the same reason, verified
        // the same way against its own battery. This ignore is scoped
        // to those two methods.
        "CastInt": {
            "ignore": [
                "Zef\\Framework\\Health\\HealthScrapeClock::__construct",
                "Zef\\Framework\\Http\\ErrorRequestContext::__construct",
                // The request authority's port bounds compare digit
                // strings against integers, and PHP compares numeric
                // strings numerically: the port grammar admits only
                // digits, so the string and its (int) cast agree on
                // every comparison the guard can ever evaluate
                // (verified by running the adapter battery under
                // both cast removals -- the complete battery passes,
                // the 0/1/65535/65536 boundary series included).
                "Zef\\Framework\\Middleware\\RequestAuthority::host",
                // The ledger probe in RoadmapRecordedMerge::isAccepted()
                // asks isset() on a map flipped from list<int>, whose keys
                // are integers, and PHP canonicalises a canonical decimal
                // numeric string to that very integer key -- so
                // isset($map['532']) and isset($map[532]) address one key
                // for every probe the trailing "(#<number>)" pattern can
                // produce (decimal digits alone, no sign, no whitespace,
                // always canonical; verified against the engine, the
                // complete suite passes under the manually applied
                // mutation). The cast exists for Psalm's
                // InvalidArrayOffset, which refuses a string offset on an
                // integer-keyed map, not for runtime semantics.
                "Zef\\Test\\Support\\Tooling\\RoadmapRecordedMerge::isAccepted"
            ]
        },
        // The Exception suffix strip in ZefException::defaultErrorCode()
        // is the one string cast in the error lane that is value-equivalent
        // under the CastString mutator: preg_replace() answers null only on
        // a PCRE error, and the fixed literal pattern ('Exception$' against
        // a class name) cannot reach one, so the operand is a string for
        // every value the line can ever see and the cast is the analyser's
        // narrowing, never a runtime coercion (verified by running the
        // exception battery under the manually applied mutation -- the
        // complete battery passes, including the multibyte-namespace and
        // overlong-name fixtures). This ignore is scoped to that single
        // source line.
        "CastString": {
            // The staging name's pid cast: the concat of an int operand
            // stringifies with the identical bytes the explicit cast
            // spells, so removing the cast answers the same staging path
            // for every pid the engine can hand the writer. The name's own
            // composition stays fully under its mutators: the battery pins
            // the basename, the pid digits, the entropy width and the
            // .staging suffix against every rearrangement. Scoped to
            // those two staging methods -- the route tree cache's dump
            // adopted the configuration cache's staging doctrine in the
            // round-2 audit, and the cast's equivalence is the doctrine's
            // own, not the file's.
            "ignore": [
                // The driver word's scalar cast: the refusal message
                // concatenates the operand PHP would coerce itself, so
                // removing the cast answers the identical message for
                // every scalar the reading can name (int 5, float 5.0,
                // bool -- the concat and the cast spell the same bytes;
                // the non-scalar arm answers get_debug_type under both).
                // The driver grammar itself stays fully under its
                // mutators: the battery pins the accepted word, the
                // unknown word, the int and the missing spellings.
                // Scoped to that single reading fold, the staging
                // doctrine's own equivalence.
                "Zef\\Framework\\Adapters\\Database\\ConnectionFields::readDriver",
                "Zef\\Framework\\Config\\Cache\\ConfigCache::stagingName",
                "Zef\\Framework\\Config\\Routing\\RouteTreeCache::stagingName"
            ],
            "ignoreSourceCodeByRegex": [
                "\\$shortName = \\(string\\) preg_replace\\('/Exception\\$/'.*",
                // The insert row's and the update pair's expression cells
                // carry the same cast: each result only ever reaches
                // implode() or concat, and both coerce a Stringable
                // object with the identical bytes the explicit cast
                // spells, so removing the cast answers the same
                // statement for every expression cell the payload
                // validation can pass (SqlExpression is Stringable by
                // its own contract). The casts are the analyser's
                // narrowing over what rector's concat-autocast removal
                // and psalm's implicit-cast refusal both accept -- the
                // assignment spelling is the one intersection. This
                // ignore is scoped to those source lines.
                "\\$rowParams\\[\\] = \\(string\\) \\$value;",
                "\\$expression = \\(string\\) \\$value;"
            ]
        },
        // The two anchored quality grammars in AcceptHeaderParser carry
        // the /D flag whose removal is unobservable by construction: both
        // operands are trimmed before the match (mb_trim on the whole
        // part, trim on every segment), and a trailing newline is the only
        // byte a non-D dollar anchor would admit, so the trimmed operand
        // answers identically under both spellings (verified by running
        // the parser battery under the manually applied mutation -- the
        // complete battery passes). These ignores are scoped to those two
        // source lines.
        //
        // The error-code fold's leading-letter probe in
        // ZefException::defaultErrorCode() carries a /D flag the pattern
        // itself cannot use: DOLLAR_ENDONLY constrains only a dollar
        // metacharacter, and the probe's pattern is caret-anchored with no
        // dollar at all, so both spellings admit exactly the same matches
        // over every string the fold can ever hand the line (verified by
        // running the exception battery under the manually applied
        // mutation -- the complete battery passes, the FRAMEWORK_ERROR
        // fallback fixtures included). This ignore is scoped to that
        // single source line.
        "PregMatchRemoveFlags": {
            "ignoreSourceCodeByRegex": [
                ".*explode\\(';', \\$part, 2\\)\\[0\\].*",
                ".*preg_match\\('/\\^\\(\\?:0.*",
                ".*preg_match\\('/\\^\\[A-Z\\]/D'.*",
                // Every router-lane pattern the flag mutator can
                // reach anchors its end with \z (the absolute
                // end) and carries no dollar metacharacter, so
                // DOLLAR_ENDONLY constrains nothing either spelling
                // can match -- the ZefException probe entry's
                // construction, held by the host grammar, the
                // authority grammar, the produces grammar, the
                // version grammar and the quality grammar alike.
                ".*\\\\z/D'.*",
            ]
        },
        // The divergence judgement's NaN guards in
        // PrometheusNumbers::diverged() are value-equivalent under
        // every spelling the mutator can write, which is the point of
        // their construction: with a NaN operand every float
        // comparison answers false, so the guard's return-false and
        // the fall-through's abs() comparison answer the identical
        // false, the LogicalOr-to-LogicalAnd flip and the guard's own
        // removal included (KDD-OB31's record: the explicit guards
        // close the reference's documented inverted-form trap -- the
        // auto-fixer's own early-exit rewrite was caught mid-PR by the
        // NaN series test, and the mutant here is the same proof
        // replayed). This ignore is scoped to that single method.
        "LogicalOr": {
            "ignore": [
                // The request version's null-or-empty arm is owned
                // by the caller: RouteVersionSelection guards null and
                // blank spellings before it consults the fold, so the
                // arm is unreachable through the public flow, and the
                // reachable spellings answer identically either way
                // (a blank trim canonicalises to the empty refusal,
                // whose catch answers the same null -- verified by
                // running the version battery under the flip, the
                // malformed-header miss included).
                "Zef\\Framework\\Config\\Routing\\RouteVersion::request",
                "Zef\\Framework\\Telemetry\\PrometheusNumbers::diverged"
            ],
            "ignoreSourceCodeByRegex": [
                // The opened-file guard's link-device merge is subsumed by
                // the inode clause that stays live: lstat() answers the
                // SYMLINK's own inode while the stream's fstat() answers
                // the target's, and no engine-openable stream can carry
                // the link's own inode -- so every input the merged
                // clause would let through the device equality (a link
                // whose target shares its filesystem) still dies on the
                // inode inequality two clauses later (verified by
                // running the security battery under the manually
                // applied mutation -- the complete battery passes, the
                // vanished-path refusal still fires one warning deep).
                // The neighbouring array guards stay under the mutator:
                // their fallthrough reads the boolean as an array
                // offset and the warning ledger tells the two apart.
                "\\|\\| \\$opened\\['dev'\\] !== \\$current\\['dev'\\]"
            ]
        },
        // The document-root resolvers' empty-string arm is the one
        // boolean the value flip cannot observe: the arm's literal is
        // read through is_string() alone -- once by the resolvability
        // guard the empty document root itself short-circuits closed,
        // and once by the containment guard, and a flipped true is
        // exactly as non-string as the original false there -- so both
        // resolvers answer the identical verdict for every runtime
        // with or without a document root (verified by running both
        // batteries under the manually applied mutations -- the
        // complete batteries pass, including the document-root-set
        // refusals that never reach the arm). The regex form cannot
        // express this: the arm's own line is a bare ": false;" that
        // a health check's narrowing arm shares, and that shared arm
        // is observably false for every non-boolean answer, so the
        // ignore is scoped to the three resolver methods instead, each
        // of which carries exactly one boolean literal. The storage
        // layout's own document-root fold (the route map's slice) joins
        // the same list with the same argument: its arm is the very
        // spelling the JSON log destination's fold owns, replayed
        // under the Storage layer, and its battery rides the same
        // document-root geometry the storage root's own guard pins.
        "FalseValue": {
            "ignore": [
                "Zef\\Framework\\Http\\ReplayStorageDirectory::resolve",
                "Zef\\Framework\\Logging\\JsonLogDocumentRoot::assertDestinationIsOutside",
                "Zef\\Framework\\Storage\\StorageDocumentRoot::assertOutside"
            ]
        },
        // The TYPE bookkeeping's boolean stores are
        // truthiness-free by construction: the bookkeeping set is
        // read through isset() alone, and isset() answers on
        // null-ness, never on truthiness -- a false-valued entry is
        // as present as a true-valued one, so the TrueValue flip
        // cannot change which TYPE headers the render emits (verified
        // by running the family suite under the manually applied
        // mutations -- the once-per-metric tests still pin both
        // bookkeeping lines). These ignores are scoped to the two
        // bookkeeping stores alone.
        "TrueValue": {
            "ignoreSourceCodeByRegex": [
                // The host grammar's duplicate-parameter witness is
                // value-blind: the duplicate check reads isset() only,
                // which answers the same question for true and false
                // alike, so the stored flag's polarity is the
                // analyser's documentation, never an observable
                // distinction (verified by running the grammar battery
                // under the flipped polarity -- the complete battery
                // passes, the repeated-label refusal included).
                ".*\\$seenParameters\\[\\$parameter] = true.*",
                "\\$emittedTypes\\[\\$metricName( \\. '_sum')?\\] = true;"
            ]
        },
        // The series count's float cast in
        // PrometheusRenderer::render() is value-equivalent by the
        // engine's own contract: PHP widens int to float silently even
        // under strict types (an int is exactly representable as a
        // float), so the cast is the analyser's narrowing of the
        // snapshot's int|float count, never a runtime coercion --
        // rendered(2) and rendered(2.0) answer the identical JSON form
        // (verified by running the family suite under the manually
        // applied mutation -- the complete suite passes, the pure
        // integer counter still renders its 3.0). The count's own
        // arithmetic in MeterCount::advanced() is the same class of
        // cast: the strict-binary-operands analysis refuses the
        // int|float mix uncast, and the trailing join only runs when
        // at least one operand is already float (the nested is_int
        // guards return the int-int case ahead of it), so the casts
        // are the analyser's narrowing of the int|float stubs, never
        // runtime coercions (verified by running the family suite
        // under the manually applied mutation -- the complete suite
        // passes, the fractional join still carries its 3.5). These
        // ignores are scoped to those two places.
        "CastFloat": {
            // The coverage percentage's two operand casts in
            // CoverageReport::percentage() join the same family against
            // the same analysis: the strict-binary-operands policy
            // refuses the int-operand mix uncast, and PHP's division
            // operator always answers float, so every runtime
            // observable coerces identically without either cast
            // (verified by this zone's own escaped pair -- the full
            // suite passes under each removed cast; the equivalence is
            // engine-level). The ignore is scoped to that single method.
            "ignoreSourceCodeByRegex": [
                "\\$count = \\(float\\) \\$series\\['count'\\];"
            ],
            "ignore": [
                "Zef\\Framework\\Telemetry\\MeterCount::advanced",
                "Zef\\Test\\Support\\Tooling\\CoverageReport::percentage"
            ]
        },
        // The render's series ceiling in
        // PrometheusRenderer::render() is a terminal loop bound: the
        // series counter is monotonic, so every iteration past the
        // ceiling answers the same too-many judgement -- a break and a
        // continue select the identical remaining iterations (nothing
        // after the first over-ceiling series appends a line either
        // way, verified by running the ceiling test under the manually
        // applied mutation -- the 4096-series render still answers
        // exactly its ceiling). The ignore is method-scoped because
        // the mutator lands on the break statement itself: a regex on
        // the guarding condition can never meet the mutated line. This
        // ignore is scoped to that single method.
        "Break_": {
            "ignore": [
                "Zef\\Framework\\Telemetry\\PrometheusRenderer::render"
            ]
        },
        // The unknown-key fold's array_values rewrap is
        // value-equivalent by the key domain it reads: array_keys()
        // answers int|string keys only, array_diff preserves them with
        // gaps, and every consumer downstream -- the empty-list guard
        // and the refusal's own implode -- reads the values in order
        // and ignores the keys, so the re-indexing the rewrap performs
        // is invisible to every observable the fold carries (verified
        // by running the changed-set under the escaped mutation -- the
        // complete mode battery passes, the gap-keyed refusals
        // included). The rewrap exists for the static contract: the
        // refusal's parameter promises a list, and the analysers hold
        // the fold to it. This ignore is scoped to that single method.
        "UnwrapArrayValues": {
            "ignore": [
                "Zef\\Framework\\ContainerModeSpellings::verdict",
                // The version selection's re-wraps are key-only
                // refinements: every consumer of its results walks
                // them with foreach (first-match-wins) and unions
                // their methods de-duplicated, so the gap-keyed map
                // the unwrap would leave behind answers the same
                // matches and the same sorted method list -- the
                // array_values call is the list contract's spelling,
                // never an observable distinction (verified by
                // running the engine battery under all four unwraps
                // -- the complete battery passes).
                "Zef\\Framework\\Config\\Routing\\RouteVersionSelection::select"
            ]
        },
        // The version selection's no-versioned branch filter is the
        // identity under its own precondition: the branch runs only
        // when no route carries a version, so filtering for the
        // unversioned keeps every candidate either way, and the
        // unwrap answers the same list (verified by running the
        // engine battery under the unwrap -- the complete battery
        // passes, the unversioned-fallback series included).
        "UnwrapArrayFilter": {
            "ignore": [
                "Zef\\Framework\\Config\\Routing\\RouteVersionSelection::select"
            ]
        },
        // The absolute URL's host-value intersection feeds a render
        // that replaces only the names the host pattern declares:
        // the extra path parameters the unwrap would forward are
        // ignored verbatim (host and path parameter names cannot
        // collide -- the compile-time binding refuses it), so the
        // rendered host is byte-identical either way (verified by
        // running the reverse-routing battery under the unwrap --
        // the complete battery passes, the tenant series included).
        "UnwrapArrayIntersectKey": {
            "ignore": [
                "Zef\\Framework\\Config\\Routing\\RouteUrlGenerator::generateAbsolute"
            ]
        },
        // The method-claim key's separator arithmetic cannot create
        // a false duplicate: the host, version and path charsets all
        // exclude the pipe byte, so dropping or appending the
        // separator stays injective over the reachable keys -- two
        // routes collide under the rewritten key exactly when they
        // collide under the original (verified by running the claim
        // battery under both rewrites -- the complete battery passes,
        // the duplicate-pattern and ambiguity refusals included).
        "Concat": {
            "ignoreSourceCodeByRegex": [
                ".*\\$canonical = \\(\\$host \\?\\? '\\*'\\).*"
            ]
        },
        "ConcatOperandRemoval": {
            "ignoreSourceCodeByRegex": [
                ".*\\$canonical = \\(\\$host \\?\\? '\\*'\\).*"
            ]
        },
        // The unknown-key refusal's strval map is value-equivalent by
        // the same key-domain argument its caller's rewrap carries:
        // the unknown list holds int|string keys only, and implode's
        // implicit scalar cast agrees with strval on both domains, so
        // unwrapping the map renders the identical refusal message for
        // every unknown list the grammar can produce (verified by
        // running the changed-set under the escaped mutation -- the
        // complete mode battery passes, the integer-keyed list refusal
        // included). The map exists for the static contract: the
        // implode call the refusal spells wants a list of strings, and
        // the analysers hold the fold to it. This ignore is scoped to
        // that single method.
        "UnwrapArrayMap": {
            "ignore": [
                "Zef\\Framework\\ContainerModeRefusals::unknownKeys"
            ]
        },
        // The unpack guard -- shared by the JSONL record writer and the
        // replay snapshot writer, which adopted the byte-walk idiom in
        // the round-2 audit -- is armour for a shape the loop's own
        // bound already excludes: the while condition's isset() keeps
        // every offset strictly inside the payload, and unpack('a*',
        // ..., offset) answers the array with the string tail at key 1
        // for every in-range offset -- so both LogicalAnd rearrangements
        // of the guard answer the same truth on every input the loop can
        // reach, and the false branch's empty remainder never runs
        // (verified by construction: no short-circuit spelling can make
        // is_array($unpacked) false while isset($unpacked[1]) reads a
        // real key). Scoped to those two methods.
        //
        // The rendering fold's closure-arm guard in
        // AotEntriesBlock::rendered() joins them for its own reason: the
        // conjunction exists for the two analysers' disagreement about
        // the entries map's shape (psalm merges the union's optional
        // members and demands the closure key's presence be judged;
        // phpstan narrows the union by the kind literal and would flag
        // any presence-question asked after it -- the conjunct order and
        // the captured member are the one spelling both accept), while
        // the runtime itself can never distinguish the arms: the
        // dumper's own data always carries a string closure member on
        // the closure kind and never carries one on the build kind, so
        // every input the fold ever meets answers both conjuncts
        // identically. A test that fed the distinguishing shape to the
        // fold would have to spell an input the fold's own declared
        // contract excludes -- pinning the analyser scaffolding at the
        // cost of the contract's honesty. The neighbouring kind
        // comparison in the fold stays under the mutator: its flip is
        // killed by the closure-entry rendering test. Scoped to that
        // single method.
        "LogicalAnd": {
            "ignore": [
                "Zef\\Framework\\Logging\\JsonLineRecordWriter::write",
                "Zef\\Framework\\Http\\ReplaySnapshotWriter::writeAll",
                "Zef\\Framework\\Container\\Aot\\AotEntriesBlock::rendered"
            ]
        },
        // The workflow collector's absent-directory early return
        // carries the freshly initialised empty map -- the walk has
        // collected nothing yet -- and the mutator's slice of an empty
        // list is the same empty list, so both spellings answer the
        // same empty collection for every root without a workflow
        // directory. The iteration itself stays under its mutators.
        // Scoped to that single method.
        "ArrayOneItem": {
            "ignore": [
                "Zef\\Test\\Support\\Tooling\\WorkflowFileCollector::collect"
            ]
        },
        // The having fold's param accumulation spreads a fragment whose
        // grammar answers exactly one value: QueryPredicate::comparison
        // -- the one fragment constructor QueryHaving::comparison() ever
        // calls -- wraps its single bound value, so the spread's second
        // and later items never exist and taking only the first element
        // answers the identical param list for every reachable state
        // (the accumulation itself IS observable and killed: the
        // two-having battery pins both params in call order). The
        // spread stays because the fragment contract carries a list,
        // and the between/like forms the having family eventually
        // wants will hand it more than one -- the mutator's slice is
        // the shape that would silently drop them, which is exactly why
        // the spelling keeps the full spread. This ignore is scoped to
        // that single method.
        "SpreadOneItem": {
            "ignore": [
                "Zef\\Framework\\Database\\QueryHaving::comparison"
            ]
        }
    },
    "minMsi": 100,
    "minCoveredMsi": 100,
    "ignoreMsiWithNoMutations": false,
    "threads": "max",
    "timeout": 10
}
